ousness and nasty remarks also affect crew cooperation. A's egocentric and communication difficulty also affect crew cooperation. D's hot temper and know-it-all also affect crew cooperation. All have concerns.\n\nWe need to weigh strengths vs concerns. C's strengths are highly relevant: anticipating dangerous situations (stress resilience, decision quality), sees complex connections (technical competence), spatial vision (attention), leadership (crew cooperation). Concerns: show-off, not open to new ideas (communication, adaptability). B's strengths: conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), weather assessment (technical), computer skills (technical). Concerns: pretentious, nasty remarks (communication). A's strengths: quick correct decisions (decision quality), calm in crisis (stress resilience), understands complicated technology (technical). Concerns: difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). D's strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), concentration, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: hot temper, know-it-all (communication, crew cooperation).\n\nWhich candidate has the best balance? C seems strongest on technical and leadership, but concerns about openness may be mitigated. B's concerns are more about tone, could be mitigated. A's communication issues may be more severe. D's hot temper is serious.\n\nThus likely C remains best. As Agent 2, we could either support C or propose further discussion. Since we need to make a recommendation, we can either keep C or shift. Let's support C, but also bring up A and D to ensure coverage. We can say we still think C is best, but we need to discuss A and D to ensure coverage. Provide some analysis.\n\nThus public message: I agree with Agent 1 that C is strongest, but we need to consider A and D. Provide evidence for A and D, compare, and reaffirm C.\n\nMetadata vote: Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
